= 1G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=1G}

Linearity of the <integral> in each argument gives a <bilinear form>, and $fg=gf$ gives <symmetry>. Also $(f,f)=\int_{-1}^1 f(x)^2dx\geq0$. If a <polynomial> $f$ is nonzero somewhere on this interval, <continuity> makes $f^2$ strictly positive on a smaller interval, so the <integral> is positive. A <polynomial> vanishing on the whole interval is the zero <polynomial>. Thus the form is <positive-definite> and is an <inner product>.

Apply the <Gram-Schmidt process> to $1,x,x^2$. Odd <functions> have zero <integral>, so $x$ is already <orthogonal> to $1$, and the third <orthogonal polynomial> is $x^2-1/3$. Their squared <norms> are
$$
 \|1\|^2=2,\qquad \|x\|^2=\frac23,\qquad
 \|x^2-1/3\|^2=\frac25-\frac49+\frac29=\frac8{45}.
$$
Therefore an **orthonormal basis** is
$$
 \boxed{\left\{\frac1{\sqrt2},\ \sqrt{\frac32}\,x,\ \sqrt{\frac58}\,(3x^2-1)\right\}.}
$$
These are the first three normalized <Legendre polynomials>. Their distinct degrees also prove that they span the three-dimensional <vector space>.

= 2E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= Solution
{parent=2E}

The <group presentation> gives the <quotient group> $\mathbb Z^3$ by the subgroup generated by the rows of
$$
 M=\begin{pmatrix}3&6&3\\0&9&9\\-3&3&6\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For the <Smith normal form>, the first <determinantal divisor> is the greatest common divisor of all entries, namely $3$. The nonzero two-by-two <matrix minors> are $\pm27$, so the second <determinantal divisor> is $27$. The third row is the second minus the first, and a nonzero minor shows the <rank> is exactly two. Consequently the nonzero invariant factors are $3$ and $27/3=9$, with one free generator. The <fundamental theorem of finitely generated abelian groups> gives
$$
 \boxed{G\cong\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/9\mathbb Z.}
$$
An element killed by $3$ has zero free coordinate, arbitrary coordinate in $\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z$, and coordinate $0,3$ or $6$ in $\mathbb Z/9\mathbb Z$. There are nine such elements. Removing the identity leaves **eight elements of order three**.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis II}

= Solution
{parent=3F}

A <contraction mapping> $T$ on a <metric space> satisfies $d(Tx,Ty)\leq qd(x,y)$ for all $x,y$, with one constant $0\leq q<1$. The <contraction mapping theorem> says that on a nonempty <complete metric space> such a map has a unique <fixed point>; successive iterates from any starting point converge to it. More precisely, if $x_n=T^nx_0$, then $d(x_n,x_*)\leq q^nd(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)$.

The <Volterra integration operator> maps <continuous functions> to <continuous functions>. In the <uniform norm>,
$$
 \|Af-Ag\|_\infty\leq\|f-g\|_\infty.
$$
This constant cannot be decreased: for $f=1$ and $g=0$, $Af(x)=x$, and both <uniform norms> are one. Hence **$A$ is not a contraction**.

Changing the order of <integration> over the triangular region gives
$$
 A^2f(x)=\int_0^x\int_0^t f(s)\,ds\,dt
 =\int_0^x(x-s)f(s)\,ds.
$$
Thus
$$
 \|A^2f-A^2g\|_\infty\leq\sup_{0\leq x\leq1}\frac{x^2}{2}\|f-g\|_\infty
 =\frac12\|f-g\|_\infty.
$$
**$A^2$ is a contraction, with sharp constant $1/2$.** This is an example of an <iterated contraction>.

= 4G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=4G}

For an <entire function>, the <Cauchy derivative formula> on a positively oriented circle of radius $R$ about $z_0$ is
$$
 \boxed{f^{(n)}(z_0)=\frac{n!}{2\pi i}\oint_{|\zeta-z_0|=R}
 \frac{f(\zeta)}{(\zeta-z_0)^{n+1}}\,d\zeta,\qquad n=0,1,\ldots.}
$$
The resulting <Cauchy estimate> is $|f^{(n)}(z_0)|\leq n!M_R/R^n$, where $M_R$ is the maximum of $|f|$ on that circle.

The <Liouville theorem> says that a bounded <entire function> is constant. Indeed, if $|f|\leq M$ on the plane, the <Cauchy estimate> with $n=1$ gives $|f'(z_0)|\leq M/R$. Sending $R$ to infinity gives $f'(z_0)=0$ at every point, hence $f$ is constant.

For the growth assumption, apply the <Cauchy estimate> on circles centred at zero. For every integer $n>k$,
$$
 |f^{(n)}(0)|\leq n!R^{k-n}\longrightarrow0\quad(R\longrightarrow\infty).
$$
All sufficiently high <Taylor series> vanish. Since the <Taylor series> of an <entire function> converges everywhere, **$f$ is a polynomial**, of degree at most $\lfloor k\rfloor$ when $k\geq0$. If a negative finite exponent is interpreted for nonzero $z$, the same estimate forces every coefficient to vanish, giving $f=0$.

= 5D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= i
{parent=5d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Away from zero the <Dirac delta distribution> is absent. On either half-line, use $u=1+k|z|$ and the <power-law ansatz> $\psi=u^\alpha$. The <Euler-Cauchy equation> becomes
$$
 \alpha(\alpha-1)-\frac{15}{4}=0,
 \qquad \alpha=\frac52\ \text{or}\ -\frac32.
$$
The growing term is excluded by boundedness at either end. Thus the independently bounded half-line solutions are
$$
 \boxed{\psi(z)=C_+(1+kz)^{-3/2}\quad(z>0),\qquad
 \psi(z)=C_-(1-kz)^{-3/2}\quad(z<0).}
$$

= ii
{parent=5d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The sign of the <Dirac delta distribution> in the equation is positive after the outer minus sign is expanded. Integrating through zero, the nonsingular potential contributes a term tending to zero, whereas the delta term contributes $3k\psi(0)$. The <jump condition for an impulse> is therefore
$$
 \boxed{\psi'(0^+)-\psi'(0^-)=-3k\psi(0).}
$$
<Continuity> at zero gives $C_+=C_-=C$. The two one-sided derivatives are $-3kC/2$ and $+3kC/2$, whose difference is exactly $-3kC$. Hence the matching condition introduces no further restriction on $C$, and the globally bounded solution is
$$
 \boxed{\psi(z)=C(1+k|z|)^{-3/2},\qquad C\in\mathbb R\ \text{or}\ \mathbb C.}
$$
This continuous solution has precisely the derivative jump required for its second <distributional derivative> to cancel the delta term. Its amplitude remains arbitrary because no normalization has been imposed.

= 6A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=6A}

For a normalized state and a <self-adjoint operator> $Q$ with finite second moment, the <quantum uncertainty> is defined by $\Delta Q=\|(Q-\langle Q\rangle)\psi\|$. Since $\langle Q\rangle$ is real,
$$
 (\Delta Q)^2=\langle(Q-\langle Q\rangle)^2\rangle
 =\boxed{\langle Q^2\rangle-\langle Q\rangle^2}.
$$
In an energy <eigenstate> of the <quantum harmonic oscillator>,
$$
 E=\langle H\rangle
 =\frac{(\Delta p)^2+\langle p\rangle^2}{2m}
 +\frac{m\omega^2}{2}\bigl((\Delta x)^2+\langle x\rangle^2\bigr).
$$
Discarding the nonnegative squared means proves the stated lower bound. The <Heisenberg uncertainty principle> is $\Delta x\,\Delta p\geq\hbar/2$, following from $[x,p]=i\hbar$. Applying the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> to the two variance contributions gives
$$
 E\geq\frac{(\Delta p)^2}{2m}+\frac{m\omega^2(\Delta x)^2}{2}
 \geq\omega\Delta p\Delta x\geq\boxed{\frac{\hbar\omega}{2}}.
$$
Here $\omega>0$ is the oscillator frequency. The bound is the ground-state energy; equality requires zero means and the appropriate minimum-uncertainty width.

= 7A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= Solution
{parent=7A}

Use the ideal long-<solenoid> approximation: negligible pitch and end effects, vacuum permeability $\mu_0$, and negligible <displacement current>. By cylindrical symmetry the <magnetic field> is axial. An <Ampère's law> rectangular loop with one axial side inside and the other outside gives $B_{\rm in}-B_{\rm out}=\mu_0NI$. A loop wholly outside shows the outside axial field is constant with radius; requiring it to vanish far away gives
$$
 \boxed{\mathbf B_{\rm in}=\mu_0NI\,\widehat{\mathbf z},\qquad
 \mathbf B_{\rm out}=0.}
$$
The axial sign is determined by the winding direction and the <right-hand rule>. The small axial component of current from the spiral pitch is neglected in this idealization.

There are $Nl$ turns, and the <magnetic flux> through each is $\pi a^2\mu_0NI$. The flux linkage is $LI$, so the <self-inductance> is
$$
 \boxed{L=\mu_0N^2\pi a^2l.}
$$
The induced <electromotive force> opposes increasing current by <Faraday's law>. Together with <Ohm's law>, the circuit equation is $L\dot I+RI=\mathcal E_0$. Solving this first-order <ordinary differential equation> with $I(0)=0$ gives
$$
 \boxed{I(t)=\frac{\mathcal E_0}{R}\left(1-e^{-Rt/L}\right),\qquad t\geq0.}
$$
The <time constant> is $L/R$, and the limiting current is $\mathcal E_0/R$.

= 8C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=8C}

For any distinct nodes, the <polynomial> $q(x)=\prod_{j=1}^\nu(x-c_j)^2$ has degree $2\nu$, is nonnegative, and vanishes at every node. The <quadrature rule> sum for $q$ is zero, but $\int_0^\pi wq\,dx>0$ because the weight is positive and $q$ is positive except at finitely many points. Therefore **no such rule is exact for all polynomials of degree at most $2\nu$**, irrespective of the chosen weights.

For the sine weight, its first four <moments> are $2,\pi,\pi^2-4,\pi^3-6\pi$. A monic degree-two <orthogonal polynomial> is
$$
 p_2(x)=x^2-\pi x+2,
$$
since direct use of these <moments> gives $\int p_2\sin x\,dx=\int xp_2\sin x\,dx=0$. Its roots lie in $(0,\pi)$ and are
$$
 \boxed{c_{1,2}=\frac\pi2\mp\sqrt{\frac{\pi^2}{4}-2},\qquad b_1=b_2=1.}
$$
The weights follow by requiring exactness for $1$ and $x$: $b_1+b_2=2$ and $b_1c_1+b_2c_2=\pi$, and the symmetric nodes imply both weights are one. To prove cubic exactness, divide any cubic as $f=ap_2+r$, with $a,r$ of degree at most one. The <integral> of $ap_2$ is zero by <orthogonality>, and its nodal values vanish; the remaining linear <polynomial> is integrated exactly. This is the <two-node Gaussian quadrature with sine weight>; the argument proves its <Gaussian quadrature> property without assuming a general quadrature theorem.

= 9H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Markov Chains}

= a
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Markov property> and the <Chapman-Kolmogorov equation> give
$$
 \mathbb P(X_{2n+2}=j\mid X_{2n}=i,X_{2n-2},\ldots,X_0)
 =\sum_{k\in S}p_{ik}p_{kj}=(P^2)_{ij}.
$$
For each intermediate state, one first uses the next-step <transition probability> and then the following one; the sampled history adds no information once $X_{2n}$ is known. Thus $W$ is a homogeneous <Markov chain> with <transition matrix> $P^2$ and initial law $\lambda$. In particular,
$$
 \boxed{\mathbb P(W_1=0)=\sum_{i\in S}\lambda_i\sum_{j\in S}p_{ij}p_{j0}.}
$$
If zero is not a state of $S$, this probability is zero.

= b
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The authoritative definition here is $Y_n=|X_n|$. On the conditioning event, $X_{n-1}=0$. Since $p_{0,1}=0$, the additional condition $Y_n=1$ then forces $X_n=-1$. The <Markov property> therefore gives
$$
 \boxed{\mathbb P(Y_{n+1}=0\mid Y_n=1,Y_{n-1}=0)=p_{-1,0}.}
$$
As usual, this <conditional probability> is asserted when the conditioning event has positive probability.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Starting from $Y_{n-2}=0$ forces $X_{n-2}=0$. The first nonzero value is consequently $X_{n-1}=-1$. Remaining nonzero at the next step forces $X_n=1$, because $p_{-1,-1}=0$. Hence, on an event of positive probability,
$$
 \boxed{\mathbb P(Y_{n+1}=0\mid Y_n=1,Y_{n-1}=1,Y_{n-2}=0)=p_{1,0}.}
$$
The two values are unequal by assumption. They expose memory of the hidden sign: immediately after leaving zero the sign is negative, whereas after two consecutive nonzero observations it is positive. Whenever the relevant observed histories coexist with positive probability, the <Markov property> for $Y$ fails, since its next-step law is not determined by its present value alone. In particular, the absolute-value projection does not preserve the <Markov property> for all initial laws; the <strong lumpability> condition would require $p_{-1,0}=p_{1,0}$.

There is a small qualification to a blanket conclusion about an unspecified initial law. The printed assumptions do not ensure that either conditioning event is possible, and a degenerate initial law may never reach the hidden states that cause the failure. For example, in the order $-1,0,1$, take
$$
 P=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad X_0=0.
$$
All printed constraints hold, yet $Y_n=0$ always and is a <Markov chain>. Thus the correct general conclusion is **the transformed process is not necessarily Markov**; the two computed probabilities give the intended failure when their histories are available.

= 10G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=10G}

The algebraic <dual space> is $V^*=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb R}(V,\mathbb R)$, the <vector space> of all real <linear functionals>. For a finite <basis> $e_1,\ldots,e_n$, define the <dual basis> by $e_i^*(\sum_j a_je_j)=a_i$, so $e_i^*(e_j)=\delta_{ij}$. Every <linear functional> satisfies
$$
 \lambda=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda(e_i)e_i^*.
$$
This equality follows by evaluating both sides on each vector of the <basis>. It proves spanning directly. If $\sum_i b_ie_i^*=0$, evaluation on $e_j$ gives $b_j=0$, proving linear independence without assuming a theorem on dual dimensions.

For a <vector subspace> $U$, its <annihilator of a vector subspace> is
$$
 U^\circ=\{\lambda\in V^*: \lambda(u)=0\text{ for all }u\in U\}.
$$
Extend a <basis> $e_1,\ldots,e_r$ of $U$ to a <basis> of $V$. Then $U^\circ$ has <basis> $e_{r+1}^*,\ldots,e_n^*$, so $\boxed{\dim U^\circ=n-\dim U}$.

The <dual map> of $\alpha:V\to W$ is $\alpha^*:W^*\to V^*$, $\lambda\mapsto\lambda\circ\alpha$. In the corresponding <dual bases> its <matrix> is the transpose of the <matrix> for $\alpha$. A <matrix> and its transpose have the same <rank>, giving $\operatorname{rank}\alpha^*=\operatorname{rank}\alpha$. Directly,
$$
 \alpha^*\lambda=0\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \lambda(\alpha v)=0\text{ for all }v
 \ \Longleftrightarrow\ \lambda\in(\operatorname{im}\alpha)^\circ.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\ker\alpha^*=(\operatorname{im}\alpha)^\circ}$. Also every $\lambda\circ\alpha$ vanishes on $\ker\alpha$, so $\operatorname{im}\alpha^*\subseteq(\ker\alpha)^\circ$. Both spaces have dimension $\operatorname{rank}\alpha$ by the preceding <annihilator of a vector subspace> formula and <rank-nullity theorem>, proving
$$
 \boxed{\operatorname{im}\alpha^*=(\ker\alpha)^\circ.}
$$
Alternatively, a <linear functional> vanishing on the kernel descends to the image of $\alpha$ and can be extended to $W$ by extending a <basis>.

For the infinite-dimensional <polynomial ring>, the coefficient functionals $L_i$ are linearly independent: evaluate a finite relation on each monomial. However, they do not span the algebraic <dual space>. The <linear functional> $p\mapsto p(1)$ takes value one on every monomial, while any finite combination of the $L_i$ vanishes on every sufficiently high-degree monomial. Thus **the $L_i$ do not form a basis of $V^*$**. An infinite formal sum of coefficient functionals is not a finite linear combination.

= 11E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= a
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With the usual commutative, unital conventions, the chain is
$$
 \boxed{\text{field}\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{principal ideal domain}
 \ \Longrightarrow\ \text{unique factorization domain}\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{integral domain}.}
$$
A <field> is an <integral domain>, and every nonzero <ideal> contains an invertible element and is consequently the whole ring. Its only <ideals> are $(0)$ and $(1)$, so it is a <principal ideal domain>.

To prove the middle implication, first show that a <principal ideal domain> has the <ascending chain condition> on <ideals>. The union of an ascending chain is an <ideal>, hence is $(a)$; its generator belongs to one member of the chain, and all later members equal $(a)$. Now suppose a nonzero nonunit admitted no factorization into <irreducible elements>. It would factor into two nonunits, at least one of which again has no such factorization. Repeating would produce a strictly increasing chain of <principal ideals>: if $a=bc$ with $c$ a nonunit, $(a)\subsetneq(b)$, since equality and cancellation would make $c$ invertible. This contradicts the <ascending chain condition>. Existence of factorization follows. By the permitted fact that every <irreducible element> is a <prime element> in a <principal ideal domain>, any irreducible factor in one factorization divides a factor in the other; cancellation then proves uniqueness up to units and order. This is a <unique factorization domain>. The final implication is part of the definition of a <unique factorization domain>.

None of the adjacent implications reverses. The <principal ideal domain> $\mathbb Z$ is not a <field>, since $2$ is not invertible. The <polynomial ring> $\mathbb Q[x,y]$ is a <unique factorization domain>, by applying the polynomial extension theorem for <unique factorization domains> twice, but its <ideal> $(x,y)$ is not principal: a generator would divide both $x$ and $y$, so would be a unit, whereas every member of this ideal vanishes at the origin. Finally, $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-5}]$ is an <integral domain> as a subring of $\mathbb C$, but is not a <unique factorization domain>. Its multiplicative <norm> is $N(a+b\sqrt{-5})=a^2+5b^2$. There is no element of norm $2$ or $3$. Hence $2,3,1+\sqrt{-5},1-\sqrt{-5}$, of norms $4,9,6,6$, are all irreducible: a nontrivial factorization would require one of those missing norms. The only units have norm one and are $\pm1$. The two factorizations
$$
 6=2\cdot3=(1+\sqrt{-5})(1-\sqrt{-5})
$$
are therefore not related by associates and reordering.

= b
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The set is the <ideal quotient> $K=(I:J)$. It contains zero. If $r,s\in K$, then $(r-s)J\subseteq I$, since $I$ is an <subgroup>. If $a\in R$ and $r\in K$, then $(ar)J=a(rJ)\subseteq I$ because $I$ is an <ideal>. Thus $K$ is an <ideal>.

Write $J=(a)$ and $K=(b)$. Because $I\subseteq J$, each $x\in I$ has the form $ar$. Then $rJ=r(aR)=xR\subseteq I$, so $r\in K$, and $x\in aK$. Conversely $aK\subseteq I$ by the definition of $K$. Therefore
$$
 \boxed{I=aK=(ab),}
$$
so $I$ is a <principal ideal>. This argument uses only the commutative ring convention, not cancellation or an <integral domain> hypothesis.

= 12F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis II}

= Solution
{parent=12F}

The <Fréchet derivative> at $p=(x_0,y_0)$ is a <linear map> $L:\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R$ for which
$$
 f(p+h)=f(p)+Lh+o(\|h\|)\quad(h\to0).
$$
This is <differentiability>; if it holds, $L(h_1,h_2)=D_1f(p)h_1+D_2f(p)h_2$.

To prove the sufficient condition, take a rectangle about $p$ contained in the open domain. Split the increment along its coordinate directions:
$$
 f(x_0+h,y_0+k)-f(p)
 =[f(x_0+h,y_0+k)-f(x_0,y_0+k)]
 +[f(x_0,y_0+k)-f(p)].
$$
The one-variable <mean value theorem>, applicable because the restrictions are differentiable, represents these two terms as
$$
 hD_1f(x_0+\theta h,y_0+k)+kD_2f(x_0,y_0+\phi k),\qquad0<\theta,\phi<1.
$$
The zero-increment cases are interpreted directly. Subtract $D_1f(p)h+D_2f(p)k$. By <continuity> of both <partial derivatives> at $p$, the remainder is at most $\varepsilon(|h|+|k|)\leq\sqrt2\varepsilon\sqrt{h^2+k^2}$ once the increment is small. This is the required little-oh remainder and proves <differentiability>.

The converse is false. Set $f(x,y)=x^2\sin(1/x)$ for $x\ne0$, and $f(0,y)=0$. At each point with $x=0$, its <Fréchet derivative> is zero, since the function increment has magnitude at most $h^2=o(\sqrt{h^2+k^2})$. It is smooth away from this line. But
$$
 D_1f(x,y)=2x\sin(1/x)-\cos(1/x)\quad(x\ne0),\qquad D_1f(0,y)=0,
$$
is not continuous there. Thus a function can be differentiable everywhere while its <partial derivatives> are discontinuous.

For $h$, every point with $x\ne0$ is a smooth point. At the origin, $|h(x,y)|\leq|xy|\leq(x^2+y^2)/2$, so $h$ is differentiable with zero derivative. At $(0,y_0)$ with $y_0\ne0$, the putative first <partial derivative> would require the limit $y_0\sin(1/x)$, which does not exist. Consequently the exact set of differentiability is
$$
 \boxed{\{(x,y):x\ne0\}\ \cup\ \{(0,0)\}.}
$$

= 13E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Metric and Topological Spaces}

= Solution
{parent=13E}

A <metric space> is <complete> if every <Cauchy sequence> in it converges to a point of that space. The stated shrinking-set characterization is the <Cantor intersection theorem> together with its converse.

First assume <completeness>, and choose $x_n\in A_n$. If $m,n\geq N$, then both points belong to $A_N$, so $d(x_m,x_n)\leq\operatorname{diam}(A_N)$. The vanishing <diameters> imply that $(x_n)$ is a <Cauchy sequence>, hence it converges to some $x\in X$. For every fixed $N$, its tail belongs to the <closed set> $A_N$, so $x\in A_N$. Thus $x\in\bigcap_N A_N$. In fact the intersection contains only one point, since any two of its points have distance at most every diameter.

Conversely assume the stated intersection property and let $(x_n)$ be a <Cauchy sequence>. Put
$$
 A_N=\overline{\{x_n:n\geq N\}}^{\,X}.
$$
These <closures> are nonempty, closed and nested. Taking a <closure> does not increase the <diameter>: approximate two points of the closure by points of the set and use <continuity> of distance. The <Cauchy sequence> therefore makes $\operatorname{diam}(A_N)\to0$. Choose $x$ in the intersection. For $n\geq N$, both $x_n$ and $x$ belong to $A_N$, and $d(x_n,x)\leq\operatorname{diam}(A_N)\to0$. Every <Cauchy sequence> converges in $X$, proving **completeness**.

= i
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the incomplete <metric space> $X=(0,1)$ with its usual distance, and
$$
 \boxed{A_n=(0,1/(n+1)]\subset X.}
$$
Each is nonempty and closed relative to $X$: it is $X\cap(-\infty,1/(n+1)]$. The sequence is strictly descending and its <diameters> are $1/(n+1)\to0$. Its intersection is empty, since every positive point exceeds $1/(n+1)$ for sufficiently large $n$. The excluded limiting point is zero.

= ii
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In $\mathbb R$, take
$$
 \boxed{A_n=(0,1/n).}
$$
These are nonempty descending sets of <diameter> $1/n$, with empty intersection. They are not <closed sets>, so there is no contradiction with the <Cantor intersection theorem> for the complete <metric space> $\mathbb R$.

= iii
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In $\mathbb R$, take
$$
 \boxed{A_n=[n,\infty).}
$$
The sets are nonempty, closed and strictly descending, but their intersection is empty. Their <diameters> are infinite, so the shrinking-diameter assumption is essential.

= 14B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=14B}

For $\operatorname{Re}s>0$, integration by parts recursively in the <Laplace transform> gives
$$
 \boxed{\mathcal L[t^n](s)=\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}},\qquad
 \mathcal L[H(t-a)](s)=\int_a^\infty e^{-st}dt=\frac{e^{-as}}s.}
$$
The initial exponential boundary term vanishes, and $\mathcal L[1]=1/s$ starts the recursion. Here $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>.

Transform the displacement in time, writing $Y(x,s)=\mathcal L_t[y](x,s)$. Zero initial displacement and velocity give $s^2Y=c^2Y_{xx}+g/s$. A particular solution is $g/s^3$, and the spatial homogeneous solutions are $e^{\pm sx/c}$. The prescribed far-field behavior excludes the growing exponential. The fixed boundary sets the remaining coefficient to $-g/s^3$, hence
$$
 Y(x,s)=\frac g{s^3}(1-e^{-sx/c}).
$$
Since $\mathcal L[t^2/2]=1/s^3$, the <Laplace transform time-shift rule> gives
$$
 \boxed{y(x,t)=\frac g2\left[t^2-H(t-x/c)(t-x/c)^2\right].}
$$
Equivalently, $y=gt^2/2$ for $t\leq x/c$, and $y=gxt/c-gx^2/(2c^2)$ for $t\geq x/c$. This solves the forced <wave equation> in each region and has continuous displacement and first derivatives across the characteristic $t=x/c$, so it also solves the equation weakly there. It satisfies the fixed-end and initial conditions and equals $gt^2/2$ sufficiently far away at each fixed time. The end's influence travels at speed $c$.

= 15F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=15F}

An <embedded surface parametrization> is a smooth map $F:U\subset\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R^3$, with open $U$, injective differential of rank two at each point, and $F$ a <homeomorphism> onto its image with the subspace topology. Its <induced metric> is
$$
 g_{ij}=\partial_iF\cdot\partial_jF,\qquad ds^2=g_{ij}du^idu^j.
$$
In coordinates, an arc-length-parametrized <geodesic> satisfies
$$
 \boxed{\ddot u^k+\Gamma^k_{ij}\dot u^i\dot u^j=0,\qquad
 g_{ij}\dot u^i\dot u^j=1,}
$$
where the <Christoffel symbols> are $\Gamma^k_{ij}=\tfrac12g^{k\ell}(\partial_i g_{j\ell}+\partial_j g_{i\ell}-\partial_\ell g_{ij})$. The normalization fixes unit speed; it is compatible with the differential equations because geodesic speed is constant.

For the cone use $F(r,\theta)=(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta,\sqrt3r)$, $r>0$, restricting $\theta$ to a suitable open interval for a chart. Direct differentiation gives its <first fundamental form>
$$
 ds^2=4\,dr^2+r^2d\theta^2.
$$
With $\rho=2r$ and $\phi=\theta/2$, this becomes $d\rho^2+\rho^2d\phi^2$, the Euclidean <metric> in <polar coordinates>. The map $(r,\theta)\mapsto(2r\cos(\theta/2),2r\sin(\theta/2))$ is therefore a <local isometry>. It is local rather than global because increasing $\theta$ by $2\pi$ increases $\phi$ by $\pi$.

Given two points, choose their angle representatives so that $|\theta_2-\theta_1|\leq\pi$. Their developed plane angles then differ by at most $\pi/2$. The straight segment between their developed positions stays away from the origin: it lies in an angular sector of width at most $\pi/2$ containing both positive-radius endpoints. Develop that segment back onto the cone. Since <local isometries> preserve <geodesics>, it gives a geodesic joining the two points; it can be parametrized by arc length. This constructs <geodesics on a punctured circular cone>. For a repeated point the constant curve is the degenerate geodesic. In particular, this argument does not appeal to completeness of the punctured cone.

**The joining geodesic need not be unique.** For endpoints with equal positive radius and opposite azimuth, one may use the angular changes $+\pi$ and $-\pi$. In the plane these give two straight segments from $(\rho,0)$ to $(0,\rho)$ and $(0,-\rho)$, respectively. Both avoid the origin. Their lifts pass on opposite sides of the cone and have distinct images, so a <reparametrization> cannot identify them.

= 16C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Variational Principles}

= Solution
{parent=16C}

Along a sufficiently smooth stationary curve, differentiation and the <Euler-Lagrange equation> give the <Beltrami identity>:
$$
 \frac d{dx}(f-y'f_{y'})=f_yy'-y'\frac d{dx}f_{y'}=0,
 \qquad\boxed{f-y'f_{y'}=\text{constant}.}
$$
The cancellation uses the absence of explicit $x$ dependence.

For an axisymmetric <surface of revolution>, its area is $I[y]=2\pi\int_{-l}^l y\sqrt{1+y'^2}\,dx$. A smooth positive-radius minimizer is stationary, so the <Beltrami identity>, with the constant factor omitted, gives
$$
 \frac{y}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=k>0.
$$
Equivalently the <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives $yy''=1+y'^2$, whence $y''=y/k^2$. The first integral selects
$$
 y=k\cosh((x-x_*)/k).
$$
Equal radii at $\pm l$ imply $x_*=0$ (subtract the two cosh values), giving
$$
 \boxed{y(x)=k\cosh(x/k),\qquad r=k\cosh(l/k).}
$$
This is a <catenoid>. The conclusion is a necessary form for a smooth connected minimizer; it does not declare every stationary catenoid a minimum.

Put $a=l/k>0$. The boundary equation is $r/l=\cosh a/a$. Its derivative is $(a\sinh a-\cosh a)/a^2$, so its unique critical point solves $a\tanh a=1$. The left side increases strictly from zero to infinity. Thus $\cosh a/a$ decreases to a unique minimum and then increases, diverging as $a\downarrow0$ and $a\to\infty$. There are exactly two positive values of $k$ when
$$
 r/l>\min_{a>0}\frac{\cosh a}{a}\simeq1.50888.
$$
At equality there is one value, and below it there is none.

To match the printed <second variation> convention, write $I[y+\varepsilon\eta]=I[y]+\varepsilon\delta I+\varepsilon^2\delta^2I+o(\varepsilon^2)$; its $\delta^2I$ is the quadratic coefficient, half the second derivative with respect to $\varepsilon$. Expanding the area integrand gives
$$
 \delta^2I=\pi\int_{-l}^l\left[\frac{y}{(1+y'^2)^{3/2}}\eta'^2
 +2\frac{y'}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}\eta\eta'\right]dx.
$$
On the <catenoid> these coefficients are $k\operatorname{sech}^2(x/k)$ and $2\tanh(x/k)$. Integrate the mixed term by parts; the boundary term is zero because $\eta(\pm l)=0$. Since $(\tanh(x/k))'=\operatorname{sech}^2(x/k)/k$, this yields
$$
 \boxed{\delta^2I=\pi\int_{-l}^l\left(k\eta'^2-\frac{\eta^2}{k}\right)\operatorname{sech}^2(x/k)\,dx.}
$$
This is the <axisymmetric second variation of a catenoid>, with the explicitly stated quadratic-coefficient convention.

= 17D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= i
{parent=17d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <complex Fourier series> of a $2L$-periodic function is
$$
 \boxed{f(x)\sim\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}c_ne^{in\pi x/L},\qquad
 c_n=\frac1{2L}\int_{-L}^L f(x)e^{-in\pi x/L}\,dx.}
$$
The coefficient formula follows from <orthogonality> of the exponential functions. For an arbitrary <square-integrable function>, this representation means convergence in the $L^2$ <norm>. With appropriate additional regularity it also gives pointwise convergence, to the mean of the one-sided limits at a jump. No pointwise representation is asserted for an unrestricted function merely from periodicity.

= ii
{parent=17d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Parseval identity> for a <complex Fourier series> is
$$
 \boxed{\frac1{2L}\int_{-L}^L|f(x)|^2dx=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}|c_n|^2.}
$$
It holds for every <square-integrable function>, with complex conjugation expressed by the absolute squares. It is the <Pythagorean theorem> extended to the complete orthonormal system of complex exponentials.

= iii
{parent=17d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Integrate the two exponentials in $\cos(\alpha x)$ against $e^{-inx}$. For $\alpha\notin\mathbb Z$ there are no zero denominators, and
$$
 c_n=\frac1{2\pi}\left[\frac{\sin\pi(\alpha-n)}{\alpha-n}
 +\frac{\sin\pi(\alpha+n)}{\alpha+n}\right]
 =\boxed{\frac{(-1)^n\alpha\sin(\pi\alpha)}{\pi(\alpha^2-n^2)}}.
$$
Thus the <complex Fourier series> is
$$
 \boxed{\cos(\alpha x)=\frac{\alpha\sin(\pi\alpha)}\pi
 \sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\frac{(-1)^ne^{inx}}{\alpha^2-n^2},\qquad -\pi\leq x\leq\pi.}
$$
This is the periodic extension, not a globally nonperiodic cosine formula. For each fixed complex $\alpha$, the coefficients are $O(n^{-2})$, giving absolute and uniform convergence. The extension is continuous at the endpoints because the two endpoint values agree; piecewise smooth <pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series> identifies the sum with it, including those endpoints.

= iv
{parent=17d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Evaluate the absolutely convergent <complex Fourier series> at $x=\pi$. The factors $(-1)^ne^{in\pi}$ equal one, and the $n=0$ term is $1/\alpha^2$. Pairing positive and negative integers gives
$$
 \cos(\pi\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sin(\pi\alpha)}\pi
 \left[\frac1{\alpha^2}-2\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^2-\alpha^2}\right].
$$
Since $\sin(\pi\alpha)\ne0$ for nonintegral complex $\alpha$, rearranging proves
$$
 \boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^2-\alpha^2}
 =\frac1{2\alpha^2}-\frac{\pi}{2\alpha\tan(\pi\alpha)}.}
$$
The last term means $\pi\cot(\pi\alpha)/(2\alpha)$, so half-integer arguments are included by the finite cotangent value zero.

= v
{parent=17d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For real nonintegral $\alpha$, the <Parseval identity> and the coefficient formula give
$$
 \frac{\alpha^2\sin^2(\pi\alpha)}{\pi^2}
 \sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\frac1{(n^2-\alpha^2)^2}
 =\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^\pi\cos^2(\alpha x)dx
 =\frac12+\frac{\sin(2\pi\alpha)}{4\pi\alpha}.
$$
Solving for the sum yields
$$
 \boxed{\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\frac1{(n^2-\alpha^2)^2}
 =\frac{\pi^2}{2\alpha^2\sin^2(\pi\alpha)}
 +\frac{\pi\cot(\pi\alpha)}{2\alpha^3}.}
$$
At $\alpha=5/2$, the sine square is one and the cotangent is zero, giving **$2\pi^2/25$**.

= 18B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=18B}

Apply <mass conservation> to a fixed horizontal region $D$. The layer mass is $\rho\int_Dh\,dx\,dy$ and the outward horizontal flux is $\rho\int_{\partial D}h(u,v)\cdot\mathbf n\,ds$. The <divergence theorem> and arbitrary choice of $D$ give
$$
 \boxed{h_t+(hu)_x+(hv)_y=0.}
$$
This depth-integrated law retains free-surface motion; neglecting the vertical velocity in horizontal momentum is not the assumption that the depth cannot change.

Take the free-surface pressure to be constant. <hydrostatic pressure> gives $p=p_{\rm atm}+\rho g(h-z)$, so the horizontal pressure forces are $-g\eta_x,-g\eta_y$. Linearization about a resting layer gives the <linearized shallow water equations>
$$
 u_t-fv=-g\eta_x,\qquad v_t+fu=-g\eta_y,\qquad
 \eta_t+h_0(u_x+v_y)=0.
$$
Taking the curl of the momentum equations yields $\zeta_t=-f(u_x+v_y)$. Combining with continuity gives the <linearized shallow-water potential-vorticity anomaly>
$$
 \boxed{\partial_t\left(\zeta-\frac f{h_0}\eta\right)=0,\qquad Q=Q_0(x,y).}
$$
For $Q_0=0$, $\zeta=f\eta/h_0$. Taking the divergence of momentum gives $\partial_t(u_x+v_y)-f\zeta=-g\nabla_h^2\eta$. Eliminate the divergence with continuity to obtain
$$
 \boxed{\eta_{tt}-gh_0\nabla_h^2\eta+f^2\eta=0.}
$$
For $\theta=k(x-ct)$, substitution of $\eta=\eta_0\cos\theta$ gives the <inertia-gravity wave> <dispersion relation>
$$
 \boxed{\omega^2=gh_0k^2+f^2,\qquad\omega=kc,\qquad c^2=gh_0+f^2/k^2.}
$$
The associated purely oscillatory <velocity field> is
$$
 u=U\cos\theta,\qquad v=V\sin\theta,\qquad
 U=\frac{c\eta_0}{h_0},\quad V=\frac{f\eta_0}{kh_0}=\frac f\omega U.
$$
These formulas satisfy all three linearized equations and the zero-anomaly condition. A separately added spatially uniform inertial oscillation is not part of this monochromatic wave.

To first order in amplitude evaluate the wave at a particle's equilibrium coordinate $x_*$, so $\theta_* = kx_*-\omega t$. Integrating its <velocity> gives displacements relative to the orbit centre
$$
 \xi=-\frac U\omega\sin\theta_*,\qquad
 \upsilon=\frac V\omega\cos\theta_*,\qquad
 \boxed{\frac{\xi^2}{(U/\omega)^2}+\frac{\upsilon^2}{(V/\omega)^2}=1.}
$$
These are <particle ellipses for rotating shallow-water waves>, with axis ratio $V/U=f/\omega<1$. For the stipulated positive signs, the velocity at phases $0,\pi/2,\pi,3\pi/2$ points respectively **right, up, left, down**, with magnitudes $U,V,U,V$. The particle orbits are clockwise as time advances, since the phase decreases.

\Image[paper-4-wave-velocities.png]
{title=Horizontal velocity directions and particle ellipse for a rotating shallow-water wave}

= 19H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=19H}

For the <normal linear model>, differentiating the <ordinary least squares> objective gives $X^TX\widehat\beta=X^TY$. Full column <rank> makes $X^TX$ invertible, so
$$
 \boxed{\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,\qquad
 \widehat\beta\sim N_p\left(\beta,\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}\right).}
$$
The distribution follows because $\widehat\beta=\beta+(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\varepsilon$ is a linear transformation of a <multivariate normal distribution>. Its <expectation> is $\beta$, and direct multiplication gives the displayed <covariance matrix>.

Let $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$, the orthogonal projection onto the design's column space. The <residual sum of squares> is $RSS=\|Y-X\widehat\beta\|^2=Y^T(I-H)Y$. Since $(I-H)X\beta=0$, symmetry and idempotence give $\mathbb E[RSS]=\sigma^2\operatorname{tr}(I-H)=(n-p)\sigma^2$. Thus
$$
 \boxed{\widehat\sigma^2=\frac{RSS}{n-p}}
$$
is an <unbiased estimator>.

For the two independent regressions choose $\beta=(a,b,c,d)^T$. The first $m$ rows of the <design matrix> are $(1,u_i,0,0)$ and the last $m$ rows are $(0,0,1,w_i)$. The centering conditions and distinct predictors give
$$
 X^TX=\operatorname{diag}(m,S_u,m,S_w),\qquad
 S_u=\sum_i u_i^2>0,\quad S_w=\sum_i w_i^2>0.
$$
It follows that
$$
 \boxed{\widehat a=\overline V,\quad\widehat b=\frac{\sum_i u_iV_i}{S_u},\quad
 \widehat c=\overline Z,\quad\widehat d=\frac{\sum_i w_iZ_i}{S_w}.}
$$
The two estimated slopes are independent <normal random variables>, as they depend on independent error blocks. Therefore
$$
 \boxed{\widehat b-\widehat d\sim N\left(b-d,\sigma^2\left(\frac1{S_u}+\frac1{S_w}\right)\right).}
$$
For these data use the pooled <residual sum of squares>
$$
 RSS=\sum_i(V_i-\widehat a-\widehat b u_i)^2
 +\sum_i(Z_i-\widehat c-\widehat d w_i)^2,\qquad s^2=\frac{RSS}{2m-4}.
$$
Its scaled value is $\chi^2_{2m-4}$ and is independent of the slope estimates by the permitted normal-model result. Hence the standardized difference has a <Student t-distribution> with $2m-4$ degrees of freedom, giving the exact **95 percent confidence interval**
$$
 \boxed{(\widehat b-\widehat d)\ \pm\ t_{2m-4,\,0.975}\,
 s\sqrt{\frac1{S_u}+\frac1{S_w}}.}
$$
Here $t_{\nu,0.975}$ is the 0.975 <quantile> of that distribution. This is a <confidence interval for the difference of independent regression slopes>. As required by the original $p<n$ framework, this specialization needs $m>2$. Distinct predictors alone allow $m=2$, but then the fit is saturated, $RSS=0$ and there are no residual degrees of freedom to estimate the unknown variance or form this interval.

= 20H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization}

= Solution
{parent=20H}

On a directed <flow network> with capacities $c_e$, choose <flows> $0\leq f_e\leq c_e$ satisfying inflow equals outflow at every vertex except source $s$ and sink $t$, and maximize the net source outflow $|f|$. For a <cut of a flow network> $S$ containing $s$ but not $t$, its capacity is $\sum_{u\in S,v\notin S}c_{uv}$. The <max-flow min-cut theorem> states
$$
 \boxed{\max_f |f|=\min_{s\in S,\ t\notin S}c(S,S^c).}
$$
Every feasible <flow> is bounded above by every cut capacity; equality for a feasible flow and a cut proves optimality.

The <Ford-Fulkerson algorithm> starts with a feasible flow, for instance zero. A forward edge has residual capacity $c_{uv}-f_{uv}$ and its reverse edge has residual capacity $f_{uv}$. Find a source-to-sink <augmenting path> in this <residual network>, and increase the flow along it by the minimum residual capacity on the path, reducing original-edge flows when reverse edges are used. Repeat until no such path exists. The vertices reachable from the source then define a saturated minimum <cut of a flow network>. With integer capacities and zero initial flow, all residual capacities and augmentations are integers. Each augmentation increases $|f|$ by at least one, while $|f|$ is bounded by the finite sum of source capacities, so termination takes finitely many steps.

For the original roads, a feasible maximizing flow is given below; every table entry is flow/capacity in vehicles per minute.

|| Road
|| Before storm
|| After storm

| $A\to R_1$
| $40/60$
| $30/60$

| $A\to R_4$
| $50/50$
| $30/50$

| $R_1\to R_3$
| $10/10$
| $10/10$

| $R_1\to R_2$
| $30/30$
| $20/30$

| $R_4\to R_2$
| $20/40$
| $0/40$

| $R_4\to R_5$
| $30/30$
| $30/30$

| $R_2\to R_3$
| $30/40$
| $20/40$

| $R_2\to R_5$
| $20/30$
| $0/30$

| $R_3\to B$
| $40/40$
| $30/40$

| $R_5\to B$
| $50/50$
| $30/50$

All road capacities are respected, and direct summation verifies <flow conservation> at each roundabout. The original flow value is $90$. The cut separating $B$ from every other vertex consists of its two incoming roads and has capacity $40+50=90$. Therefore the original maximum is **90 vehicles per minute**.

To impose a <vertex capacity>, split $R_2$ into $R_2^-$ and $R_2^+$. Send its incoming roads to $R_2^-$ and its outgoing roads from $R_2^+$, and add a directed internal edge $R_2^-\to R_2^+$ of capacity $20$. Every unit passing through the roundabout must traverse this internal edge. This gives the standard <maximum flow with vertex capacities> construction.

Starting from zero, apply the <Ford-Fulkerson algorithm> with the following <augmenting paths> in order. First use $A\to R_1\to R_3\to B$: its residual capacities are $60,10,40$, so augment by $10$. Next use $A\to R_4\to R_5\to B$: the residual capacities are $50,30,50$, so augment by $30$. Finally use $A\to R_1\to R_2^-\to R_2^+\to R_3\to B$: the residual capacities at this stage are $50,30,20,40,30$, so augment by $20$. No reverse edge is needed in this valid run. The resulting original-road flows are precisely the after-storm column above, and the internal edge carries $20$.

For termination and optimality, take the source-side set $\{A,R_1,R_4,R_2^-\}$. Its outgoing edges are $R_1\to R_3$, $R_4\to R_5$ and $R_2^-\to R_2^+$, of capacities $10,30,20$. They are all saturated, and no positive-flow original edge enters this set. Consequently no residual path leaves the set, and its cut capacity equals the constructed flow value. The new maximum is therefore
$$
 \boxed{60\ \text{vehicles per minute}.}
$$

\Image[paper-4-network-flows.png]
{title=Optimal road flows before the storm and after splitting the flooded roundabout}
